WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.287
How do heap priority queues

2
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.600
work? A min-priority queue returns

3
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.440
the smallest key first, using

4
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.700
a min-heap to make that

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.033
key easy to find. Arrival

6
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.380
order does not determine priority.

7
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.200
Our heap starts as one,

8
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.980
three, two, seven, five, four.

9
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.340
Peeking reads the root, one,

10
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.980
without removing it; this takes

11
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.300
constant time because heap order

12
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.680
guarantees that root is a

13
00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.000
minimum.

14
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.980
To insert zero, append it

15
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.380
at the next array position,

16
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.080
preserving the complete-tree shape. The

17
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.340
new leaf may be smaller

18
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.120
than its parent, so repair

19
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.040
upwards along its ancestor chain.

20
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:42.020
Zero is below two, so

21
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.580
swap zero above two. It

22
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.200
is still below one, so

23
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.300
swap again; zero is now

24
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.220
the root, and insertion is

25
00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:50.620
complete.

26
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.240
To extract the minimum, first

27
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.740
save the old root zero

28
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:56.980
as the return value. Replace

29
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.040
it with the last key,

30
00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.320
two, then shrink the active

31
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.560
array; returning the replacement would

32
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.680
be rather embarrassing.

33
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.180
The replacement two is larger

34
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.720
than its smaller child one.

35
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.320
Swap them and stop, giving

36
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.040
one, three, two, seven, five,

37
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.340
four again; the extracted record

38
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.240
remains zero.

39
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.360
Decrease-key lowers a known record,

40
00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.860
so its possible violation is

41
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.220
upwards. Lower seven to zero,

42
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.740
swap it above three, then

43
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.840
above one; an indexed heap

44
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.260
must update record locations after

45
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.020
every swap.

46
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.620
A complete binary tree has

47
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.420
logarithmic height. Sift-up and sift-down

48
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.560
cross at most that many

49
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.520
parent-child edges, doing constant work

50
00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.880
per edge, so insertion repair,

51
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.780
extraction, and known-position decrease-key each

52
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.020
take order log n.

53
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.080
The heap stores n records,

54
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.260
and an indexed version also

55
00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.660
stores their locations. A single

56
00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.280
iterative repair needs constant temporary

57
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.540
space; a recursive repair can

58
00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.700
add a logarithmic stack. Building

59
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.200
from an array takes linear

60
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:05.760
time.

61
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.760
Things to take note of.

62
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.520
Searching the whole heap for

63
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.000
a record can cost linear

64
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.180
time, so decrease-key assumes its

65
00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.400
position is already known. Heap

66
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.300
repair bounds also separate from

67
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.160
occasional array resizing costs.

68
00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:22.779
Define what extraction and peek

69
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.200
do on an empty queue.

70
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.000
A singleton extraction simply returns

71
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.720
its only record and leaves

72
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.660
an empty heap, with no

73
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:31.600
sift-down needed.

74
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.960
Insertions rise, root replacements sink,

75
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.120
and decrease-key rises from its

76
00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.240
known position. Keep whole records

77
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.120
together and save the extracted

78
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:42.920
root before overwriting it. There,

79
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.560
a queue with standards.
